\name{BaseRQqLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRQqLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Q-Q Plot Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{qqnorm()} and \code{qqplot()} as the scatter of quantile pairs they
draw.

A Q-Q plot is a scatter, and \code{BaseRPointLayerProcessor} already knows how
to emit one -- the selector, the title and the grid all carry over
unchanged, and \code{qqnorm()} exports its marks under
\code{graphics-plot-N-points-1}, the same grob the point processor looks for.
What does not carry over is the \strong{data}.

The base R processors read a call's \emph{recorded arguments}, not the drawn
grob, and a Q-Q plot's arguments are not its coordinates. \code{qqnorm(y)} is
handed one sample and draws it against theoretical quantiles it computes;
\code{qqplot(x, y)} is handed two samples of possibly different lengths and
draws one interpolated pair per point of the shorter. Read as an ordinary
scatter, both would announce numbers the chart does not draw -- for
\code{qqnorm} the raw sample on an axis of standard deviations, which is the
one reading a Q-Q plot most needs not to have, because the whole point of
the chart is the comparison between the two.

So the coordinates are not re-derived here. \code{stats} computes them and
both functions will hand them over without drawing: \code{plot.it = FALSE}
returns exactly the pairs the plotted call would have drawn. Forwarding
the recorded arguments rather than picking them apart is what makes the
awkward cases free. Measured on eight values against five:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{qqnorm(x, plot.it = FALSE)$x    theoretical quantiles, in the
                                caller's order, not sorted
qqnorm(x, datax = TRUE, ...)    the same pair, swapped
qqplot(x, y, plot.it = FALSE)   both length 5; x interpolated to the
                                shorter sample's quantiles
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{datax} is the clearest of them: it swaps which axis holds the sample,
and forwarding it means nothing here has to know that.

The pairs come back in the order the call drew them, which is the order
the \code{points} grob lays its marks down in, so the selector list keeps
pairing positionally. Nothing is sorted.
}
